\begin{frontmatter}

\title{\rev{A chance-corrected benchmark of ECG representations for unsupervised
arrhythmia structure}}

\author[uh]{Dongho Chun}
\author[af]{Axel Faes\corref{cor1}}
\cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author.}
\ead{axel.faes@uhasselt.be}
\affiliation[uh]{organization={Data Science Institute (DSI), Hasselt University}, city={Hasselt}, country={Belgium}}
\affiliation[af]{organization={Biomedical Data Sciences, Data Science Institute (DSI) and Biomedical Research Institute (BIOMED), Hasselt University}, city={Hasselt}, country={Belgium}}

\begin{abstract}
\rev{Unsupervised analysis of the electrocardiogram (ECG) promises label-free discovery of cardiac structure. How well clustering recovers clinical classes from each common family of signal representation, hand-crafted features among them, has not been compared under chance-corrected metrics. We benchmarked four representation families for unsupervised separation of sinus rhythm (SR) from atrial fibrillation (AF) on PTB-XL. The families were whole-record raw flattening, a convolutional autoencoder (conv-AE), compact hand-crafted rhythm and morphology features, and two frozen foundation-model embeddings (HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder). Evaluation was chance-corrected (adjusted Rand index, ARI) and patient-grouped, so that no patient appeared in more than one fold. It was repeated over ten seeds for the clusterers that depend on one. Each representation was bounded by a supervised ceiling, the accuracy a supervised classifier reaches on the same features. ECGFounder clustered best (ARI up to 0.885 with $k$-means), and 16 interpretable hand-crafted features were competitive (0.711 with agglomerative clustering). Raw flattening and the conv-AE clustered at chance level (ARI $\approx 0$), although the conv-AE reached a supervised ceiling of 0.678 against a majority baseline of 0.504. The ceiling of raw flattening was 0.519, barely above that baseline. On a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task, to which neither foundation model was applied, a beat-morphology feature set again clustered far better (ARI 0.783) than the conv-AE (0.127) and a raw beat window (0.049). The silhouette score rewarded the degenerate one-cluster solution (silhouette 0.81 at ARI 0.000), so it cannot validate ECG clustering alone. Feature scaling also changed the outcome: the mean ARI of the hand-crafted features fell from 0.629 under standard scaling to 0.229 under quantile scaling. These chance-corrected controls, which locate where raw flattening and the conv-AE fail, are the contribution, together with the measurement of each representation under deployment stress. Under a 9:1 SR:AF class imbalance the hand-crafted features overtook ECGFounder (ARI 0.265 against 0.063). Under federation across ten heterogeneous sites the $k$-means ARI of the hand-crafted features fell from 0.644 to 0.591, while that of ECGFounder fell from 0.885 to 0.612. The extraction of the hand-crafted features cost fifteen times less per record than that of ECGFounder.}
\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
electrocardiography \sep unsupervised clustering \sep representation learning \sep
foundation models \sep adjusted Rand index \sep PTB-XL
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

\rev{The representation fed to a clustering algorithm may decide more of the outcome of clustering the electrocardiogram (ECG) than the algorithm itself. Clustering serves the clinical questions that come without labels: which patients group together, what subtypes exist, and whether structure can be found where labels are expensive or unavailable. Clustering the records directly is the natural first attempt at such questions. A clustering algorithm sees only the distances that the representation defines, however. A representation whose dominant variation is unrelated to the classes leaves every algorithm without a class signal, while one built from the quantities that define the classes may let a standard algorithm recover them. This study therefore compares representations on the same clustering algorithms, under evaluation that corrects for chance.}

\rev{A master's project that clustered whole 12-lead records reported misleading numbers, and its conclusion is the starting point of this study. The project flattened each record into a long vector, compressed it with a dense autoencoder, and clustered the code. Its report gave a high cluster purity, the share of the majority class inside the clusters, and a silhouette score near 0.89, an index of cluster compactness computed without labels. The purity equalled the majority-class proportion in every setting, however, and the silhouette was measured on a degenerate solution in which almost all points fell into a single cluster. The project concluded that the difficulty lies in processing the raw signal rather than in the clustering step. We test that conclusion under chance-corrected evaluation, with a supervised ceiling for each representation.}

\rev{A representation can carry information about a label and still fail to cluster by it. Linear separability is how well a linear classifier trained on the labels recovers them from the representation. Clusterability is a distinct property: how well an unsupervised partition of the representation agrees with the labels. A representation may place the discriminative direction far from its dominant axes of variation. A supervised probe, a classifier trained on the labels, then recovers the label while an unsupervised partition does not. In self-supervised image models, the hidden-layer outputs of the projector scored worse on clustering metrics than the encodings. A single linear layer separates the two, and both reach nearly the same linear-probe accuracy \cite{sslclustering2025}. We treat this distinction as background rather than as a finding.}

\rev{For the ECG, how the common representation families compare on separability and clusterability under chance-corrected metrics has not been measured. The most directly related work benchmarks several ECG foundation models against one another \cite{lookingbeyond2026}. Pooled over 12-lead datasets with PTB-XL among them, it analyses how far their embeddings group by clinical label and by dataset. It notes that the earlier benchmarks it reviews included no representation-wise evaluation and focused on performance in downstream tasks. It adds no hand-crafted features, and its datasets do not include the MIT-BIH database. A comparison with hand-crafted features matters because they are interpretable and need no training, and MIT-BIH adds a beat-morphology task to the rhythm task of PTB-XL.}

\rev{We therefore benchmarked four representation families for unsupervised SR-versus-AF separation (sinus rhythm versus atrial fibrillation), with the controls the original project lacked. The ordering of the representations follows from the class definitions and is not the contribution. SR and AF differ by definition in the regularity of the rhythm, so it is expected that compact hand-crafted features and a large foundation model both recover the split. The benchmark adds what its controls reveal and how the representations behave under deployment stress. We propose no new method, and we credit the separability-versus-clusterability phenomenon to prior work. Every statement about a foundation model is scoped to the two models tested, for the reasons given in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}. The contributions are empirical:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A clustering benchmark of raw, convolutional autoencoder (conv-AE), hand-crafted and two frozen foundation-model representations. It is chance-corrected and significance-tested, and it is patient-grouped: every record of a patient stays in one fold. A supervised classifier trained on each representation sets its supervised ceiling, against which its clustering is read. We show that the ordering is stable across the assumed number of clusters.
\item Two reproducible methodological cautions that explain the original project's misleading numbers. The silhouette, an internal validity index that scores a partition from the data alone without labels, rewarded the degenerate one-cluster solution. Feature scaling, the per-feature transformation applied before clustering, changed the result: the mean adjusted Rand index (ARI) of the hand-crafted features fell from 0.629 under standard scaling to 0.229 under quantile scaling.
\item A deployment comparison under two independent stressors: a 9:1 SR:AF class imbalance, and federation across ten simulated sites whose class proportions were drawn from a Dirichlet distribution with concentration 0.3. The 16 hand-crafted features overtook ECGFounder, the stronger foundation model on balanced data, under that imbalance. Across the federated sites they kept a larger share of the ARI they reach without federation than either foundation model did.
\item A generalization check on a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task, the data\-set the closest prior benchmark omits.
\end{enumerate}
\end{revblock}

\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Unsupervised ECG and representations.} Hand-crafted features built from
RR-interval irregularity and beat morphology encode the defining signatures of common
arrhythmias and underpin decades of automatic analysis \cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}.
Tensor and wavelet decompositions have been combined with spectral clustering for
unsupervised 12-lead classification \cite{wavelettensorecg2018}. \rev{A reconstruction autoencoder is trained only to reproduce its input, and its objective holds no term that separates the classes. Its bottleneck may therefore encode a class without arranging it into clusters, as the conv-AE of this study does (Section~\ref{sec:results}).} \rev{Contrastive self-supervised methods for the ECG preceded the foundation models below. CLOCS \cite{clocs2021} builds on patient- and lead-level invariances, and 3KG \cite{threekg2021} on physiologically motivated augmentations. Contrastive predictive coding for 12-lead ECG adapts a self-supervised method from other domains \cite{mehari2022ssl}.} Self-supervised and foundation models for
ECG, such as HuBERT-ECG \cite{hubertecg2024} and ECGFounder \cite{ecgfounder2024}, learn
general representations from millions of recordings and are now evaluated as frozen
feature extractors.

\rev{We compare four representation families: raw flattening, the conv-AE, hand-crafted features and frozen foundation-model embeddings. Raw flattening and the conv-AE encode signal energy, through the sample amplitudes and the reconstruction objective. The foundation-model embeddings place each record in a feature space fixed by large-scale pretraining, and the hand-crafted features encode the quantities that define the classes. The wavelet-tensor and spectral-clustering method of \cite{wavelettensorecg2018} addresses a related unsupervised goal with a different (tensor) representation, and we leave a tensor representation to future work.}

\paragraph{Clustering evaluation.} Cluster purity is not chance-corrected: assigning
every point to the majority class attains a purity equal to that class's prevalence.
\rev{The adjusted Rand index \cite{hubertarabie1985} and adjusted mutual information \cite{vinh2010} are external, chance-corrected indices. Both are zero for a one-cluster solution, since its Rand index equals its expectation and its mutual information with the classes is zero. This property makes them the appropriate headline metrics.} Internal indices such as the silhouette
\cite{rousseeuw1987} measure compactness in the embedding being clustered and can be
maximised by degenerate solutions, so they require cluster-size sanity checks.

\section{Methods}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Data and cohorts}

\paragraph{PTB-XL.} \rev{We used PTB-XL, a public 12-lead database, in its version 1.0.3, which holds 21\,799 records from 18\,869 patients. The paper that describes the database \cite{ptbxl2020} reports 21\,837 records from 18\,885 patients. Each record holds one 10\,s signal of twelve leads, called a strip below. Rhythm labels were taken from the SCP-ECG (Standard Communications Protocol for Computer-Assisted Electrocardiography) statements. The SR-versus-AF cohort used as positives all records carrying an AF statement, AFIB (atrial fibrillation) or AFLT (atrial flutter), so atrial flutter records were counted as AF positives. Sinus-rhythm records without any AF statement served as controls.} PTB-XL encodes rhythm statements at a likelihood of zero meaning ``present'', \rev{so a presence-based rule was required, since a confidence threshold would discard every SR record. We drew a balanced, patient-disjoint cohort of 2\,542 records (1\,282 SR, 1\,260 AF), so the majority-class baseline is 0.504. Records were taken from the \mbox{100\,Hz} release of the database as signals of 1000 samples by 12 leads. No filtering, resampling or rescaling was applied before each representation's own preprocessing, so every representation started from the same signal. We chose the lead set for each representation separately, and the description of each representation below states it. Every fold of the evaluation was drawn by patient, and no patient contributed records to both classes.}

\paragraph{MIT-BIH.} \rev{For generalization we used the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database \cite{mitbih2001}, distributed through PhysioNet \cite{physionet2000}. Each annotated beat was segmented into a window of $[-300, +250]$\,ms around the R-peak across both leads. We formed a balanced normal-versus-premature-ventricular (N-vs-V) task of 3\,000 beats (1\,500 per class) from 47 records, with the record as the grouping unit, so that the beats of one record never fell in two folds. This is a morphology task and a deliberate contrast to the PTB-XL rhythm task. In PTB-XL the two classes differ in the rhythm of a 10\,s strip, whereas in MIT-BIH they differ in the shape of a single beat. The second task therefore tests whether the ordering of the representations depends on the kind of class difference.}

\subsection{Representations}

\rev{We compared four representation families on PTB-XL (Table~\ref{tab:main}):}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Raw flattening} reproduced the original project's design on all twelve leads, since that design concatenated every lead. Each lead of the 1000-by-12 signal was reduced by a non-overlapping six-sample mean to 166 samples, and the leads were concatenated into one 1992-dimensional vector ($\sim$2000 dimensions). Its features are the sample amplitudes themselves, with no clinical model imposed on the signal. It carries amplitude and energy, and nothing aligns the samples of one record in time with those of another.
\item \textbf{Convolutional autoencoder} (conv-AE): a 1-D convolutional autoencoder with a 32-unit bottleneck, trained to convergence with a validation split, on all twelve leads resampled to 12 by 2500 and z-scored per lead. It takes the place of the dense autoencoder of the original project, whose clustered code fell almost entirely into one cluster (Section~\ref{sec:intro}). Because its objective is reconstruction, the bottleneck keeps whatever carries signal energy, which need not be what separates the classes.
\item \textbf{Hand-crafted} (16 features): RR-interval statistics and irregularity, P-wave presence and amplitude, QRS width and amplitude, and beat-template consistency \cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}, extracted with NeuroKit2 \cite{neurokit2021}. The RR statistics were SDNN (the standard deviation of the RR intervals), RMSSD (the root mean square of successive RR differences), pNN50 (the proportion of successive RR differences above \mbox{50\,ms}), the coefficient of variation, RR entropy and mean heart rate. All 16 features were computed from the 1000 samples of lead II alone, on which R-peak detection and wave delineation were performed. We used lead II as the rhythm lead of the hand-crafted features, and RR regularity and P-wave presence were both read from it. Automated AF detection from a single short ECG lead was also the task of the PhysioNet/Computing in Cardiology Challenge 2017 \cite{clifford2017af}. The representation measures directly the two quantities that make up the AF signature, RR irregularity and P-wave absence.
\item \textbf{Foundation-model embeddings} (frozen): mean-pooled hidden states of HuBERT-ECG (768-d) \cite{hubertecg2024} and the 1024-d pooled features of ECGFounder \cite{ecgfounder2024}, both used as fixed feature extractors with no fine-tuning. Both took all twelve leads, the input of the 12-lead versions of the published models. HuBERT-ECG received the first \mbox{5\,s} at \mbox{100\,Hz}, flattened lead-major into a 6000-sample vector. ECGFounder received the full strip resampled to 12 by 5000 for its \mbox{500\,Hz} input, z-scored once over the whole signal. This arm asks whether a feature space fixed by large-scale pretraining places the rhythm classes in separate regions.
\end{itemize}
\end{revblock}
\rev{On MIT-BIH the rhythm features do not transfer to single beats, so a raw beat window, a 21-dimensional beat-morphology and RR-context feature set, and the conv-AE were compared. The 21-dimensional set is a separate beat-level feature set of the same kind as the 16 PTB-XL features, not the same features. Neither foundation model was applied to the 2-lead beats. The versions used take a 12-lead input, \mbox{5\,s} long for HuBERT-ECG and \mbox{10\,s} long for ECGFounder. Each MIT-BIH input is a 2-lead window around one R-peak.}

\rev{The two lead sets of the PTB-XL representations answer to different constraints. The hand-crafted features were computed from lead II alone, and raw flattening used all twelve leads because that is the design it reproduces. Raw flattening therefore received more input than the hand-crafted features, so a lack of input is unlikely to explain why its clustering stayed at chance level. The twelve-lead input also made its vector 1992-dimensional. Whether that dimensionality, rather than the content of the coordinates, limits its Euclidean clustering was not tested.}

\subsection{Clustering and evaluation}

\rev{Each representation was clustered with five algorithms of scikit-learn \cite{sklearn2011}, with the number of clusters set to the number of classes:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{itemize}
\item $k$-means, with ten restarts per seed.
\item Ward agglomerative clustering, with Ward linkage on Euclidean distance.
\item Spectral clustering, with a nearest-neighbour affinity and $k$-means label assignment.
\item A Gaussian mixture model (GMM), with diagonal covariance and a covariance floor of \mbox{$10^{-4}$}, since a full covariance is singular for the high-dimensional representations.
\item Hierarchical density-based spatial clustering of applications with noise (HDBSCAN) \cite{hdbscan2013}, with a minimum cluster size of $n/50$ rounded down and floored at 10, which is 50 records on the PTB-XL cohort.
\end{itemize}
\end{revblock}

\rev{Tolerances and iteration caps of the clusterers were the scikit-learn defaults, and no early stopping was imposed on them. Ward agglomerative clustering and HDBSCAN are deterministic given the input matrix, so each was fitted once rather than once per seed. The conv-AE was trained with Adam at a learning rate of \mbox{$10^{-3}$} and batch size 64, for at most 50 epochs on a 90/10 train/validation split. Training stopped when the validation reconstruction error had not improved for eight epochs, and the best-validation weights were kept. The two foundation models were used as frozen feature extractors with their published weights unchanged. HuBERT-ECG was mean-pooled over time from its last hidden layer at batch size 64, and the globally average-pooled 1024-dimensional feature layer of ECGFounder was taken at batch size 32.}

\rev{The headline metrics were the chance-corrected adjusted Rand index (ARI) \cite{hubertarabie1985} and adjusted mutual information (AMI) \cite{vinh2010}. Normalized mutual information (NMI), V-measure, Fowlkes-Mallows, purity, silhouette \cite{rousseeuw1987} and the largest-cluster fraction were reported alongside, and every metric was computed against a majority and a random baseline. None of the eight metrics carries a physical unit. The first five are dimensionless indices of agreement between the true partition and the recovered one, purity is the majority-class share inside the clusters, the silhouette is a dimensionless index of cluster compactness computed without labels, and the largest-cluster fraction is a proportion of the cohort. On ARI and AMI the majority baseline scores exactly 0 and the random baseline 0 in expectation, which is the chance level of the two indices. Table~\ref{tab:metrics} gives each reported metric with its definition, its range, and how a value on it is read, in this notation:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{itemize}
\item $U$ is the partition by true class and $V$ the partition by cluster.
\item $n$ is the number of records, and $n_{ij}$ the number of records in class $i$ and cluster $j$.
\item RI is the fraction of record pairs on which $U$ and $V$ agree.
\item $I$ and $H$ are mutual information and entropy, with $\bar{H}$ the arithmetic mean of $H(U)$ and $H(V)$.
\item $E[\cdot]$ is the expectation under the permutation model that holds both sets of group sizes fixed.
\item $a_k$ and $b_k$ are the mean distance from record $k$ to the rest of its own cluster and to the nearest other cluster.
\end{itemize}
\end{revblock}

\rev{A supervised ceiling bounded how much of the label a probe recovers from the same features under the same patient-grouped five-fold protocol. We reported both a linear probe (balanced logistic regression) and a non-linear probe (random forest), so that a high ceiling with a low ARI identifies a geometric rather than an informational failure. Each configuration was repeated over ten seeds, and the headline contrasts were tested with a paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test across seeds.}

\rev{Because heavy-tailed features can dominate Euclidean distance, we swept four feature-scaling methods (Yeo-Johnson power, standard, quantile, robust) and report power scaling as the default unless stated otherwise. Feature scaling was fitted on the full representation matrix, and any hand-crafted feature that a record left undefined was filled with that feature's cohort median before scaling. To check that fixing the cluster count to the number of classes does not flatter the comparison, we also report adjusted mutual information, which is comparable across cluster counts, for $K$ from 2 to 8. Because the natural SR-to-AF prevalence is imbalanced, we additionally evaluated on a 9:1 SR:AF subsample.}

\begin{table}[!ht]
\centering
\caption[Reported clustering metrics.]{\rev{Reported clustering metrics: definition, range
and how a value is read. The notation is defined in the text.}}
\label{tab:metrics}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcp{0.36\linewidth}@{}}
\toprule
Metric & Definition & Range & Reading \\
\midrule
ARI & $\dfrac{\mathrm{RI}-E[\mathrm{RI}]}{\max\mathrm{RI}-E[\mathrm{RI}]}$ & $\le 1$ & Chance-corrected pair agreement. 0 at chance, 1 for identical partitions. \\[4pt]
AMI & $\dfrac{I-E[I]}{\bar{H}-E[I]}$ & $\le 1$ & Chance-corrected shared information. 0 at chance, 1 for identical partitions. \\[4pt]
NMI & $I/\bar{H}$ & $[0,1]$ & Shared information without the chance correction; rises with the cluster count. \\
V-measure & $2hc/(h+c)$ & $[0,1]$ & Harmonic mean of homogeneity $h$ and completeness $c$; equals NMI under arithmetic normalization. \\
Fowlkes-Mallows & $\sqrt{PR}$ & $[0,1]$ & Geometric mean of pair precision $P$ and pair recall $R$; not chance-corrected. \\
purity & $\frac{1}{n}\sum_j \max_i n_{ij}$ & $[0,1]$ & Majority-class share inside clusters. A single cluster already scores 0.504 here. \\
silhouette & $\frac{1}{n}\sum_k \dfrac{b_k-a_k}{\max(a_k,b_k)}$ & $[-1,1]$ & Internal and label-free. 1 tight and separated, 0 overlapping, negative misassigned. \\[4pt]
largest-cluster frac. & $\max_j |v_j|/n$ & $[1/|V|,1]$ & Degeneracy check. 1 means one cluster holds every record. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\rev{HDBSCAN labels records it does not assign to any dense region as noise, and the metrics treated that label in two ways. ARI, AMI, NMI, V-measure and Fowlkes-Mallows received the prediction unchanged, so the noise records formed one further group of their own and were charged against the agreement with the true partition. Purity and the silhouette were computed on the assigned records only, because neither is defined for a set that is not a cluster, and the purity denominator stayed the full cohort. Noise records were never dropped from the evaluation and never reassigned to a nearest cluster. This treatment is why HDBSCAN gave the lowest ARI of the five clusterers, or shared it, on every representation. At this minimum cluster size it assigned a minority of the cohort on the representations that cluster well, and on the matrices of raw flattening, the conv-AE and HuBERT-ECG it labelled the entire cohort as noise, which leaves one group and an ARI of exactly zero.}

\rev{Uncertainty was quantified at two levels, which answer different questions. A percentile bootstrap over the ten per-seed values, \mbox{10\,000} resamples, measures how far a result moves when only the random seed moves. These intervals were narrow: across the five PTB-XL representations the $k$-means seed interval spanned less than 0.002 in ARI, and for two of them it had zero width because every seed returned the same partition. Ward agglomerative clustering and HDBSCAN have no seed interval at all, since each was fitted once. A seed interval therefore carries no more information than the seed standard deviation it would replace.}

\rev{The interval reported with the results is the second one, the patient-bootstrap interval: a patient-grouped percentile bootstrap over the evaluation cohort with \mbox{5\,000} resamples. The partitions were held fixed and the cohort was resampled with replacement at the patient level, so every record of a drawn patient travelled with it. Because the clustering was not refitted on each resample, the patient-bootstrap interval leaves out the variation that refitting the clusterer on a resampled cohort would add. The statistic resampled is the quantity the tables report, the mean ARI over the ten seeds where the clusterer is seeded and the single fit where it is not, so each interval contains its own point estimate. All intervals are the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap distribution at a fixed bootstrap seed.}

\rev{Each paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test was reported with two effect sizes. With ten paired seeds the two-sided p-value is pinned at its floor of 0.002 as soon as every seed moves the same way, so it separates none of the contrasts and cannot express a magnitude. Cliff's delta gives the dominance of one arm over the other across all cross-seed pairs, on a scale from $-1$ to $1$, and the matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation rescales the signed-rank statistic itself onto the same scale. Each headline contrast also carried the paired difference in ARI with its own paired patient-bootstrap interval.}

\subsection[Federated clustering]{\rev{Federated clustering}}

\rev{To probe deployment under data siloing we used federated $k$-means, in which the server averages the site centroids with weights, as FedAvg averages locally trained models \cite{fedavg2017}. The 2\,542 records were partitioned, patient-grouped, across ten simulated sites, either independently and identically distributed (IID) or with class proportions drawn from a Dirichlet distribution so that sites differed sharply in arrhythmia prevalence (non-IID). The Dirichlet concentration was 0.3, applied per class over the ten sites, and any site left holding fewer records than clusters was dropped. Feature scaling was fitted once on the full representation matrix before partitioning, so the federation concerned the clustering step alone. Separate sites would not share these pooled statistics, a limitation taken up in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}. Federated clustering consisted of fifteen aggregation rounds of three local Lloyd iterations per site. Before the first round the centroids were initialised globally from two randomly drawn records. In each round the server replaced each centroid by the mean of the site centroids, weighted by the number of records each site assigned to it. Both settings were repeated over ten seeds, with the site partition and the centroid initialisation redrawn at each seed. We report the global ARI relative to centralized clustering of the same representation.}

\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection[Comparison of representations and clustering algorithms on PTB-XL]{\rev{Comparison of representations and clustering algorithms on PTB-XL}}

\rev{On PTB-XL the spread of ARI across representations was larger than the spread across clustering algorithms within a strong representation, so the representation decided the outcome (Table~\ref{tab:main} and Fig.~\ref{fig:heatmap}). ECGFounder clustered best (ARI up to 0.885 with $k$-means), the hand-crafted features were competitive (0.711 with agglomerative clustering), HuBERT-ECG was weak (0.195), and raw flattening and the conv-AE clustered at chance level (ARI $\approx 0$). Among the clusterers, HDBSCAN labelled most points as noise and underperformed throughout. Spectral clustering reached 0.016 on HuBERT-ECG, against 0.174 for $k$-means and 0.195 for the Gaussian mixture on the same representation. A poorly connected nearest-neighbour affinity graph is a possible cause, and the connectivity of the graph was not measured. Both clusterers are reported with the settings given in Section~\ref{sec:methods}, and their parameters were not adjusted to the outcome.}

