conv-AE (32-d)             & 0.004 & 0.008 & 0.001 & 0.005 & 0.000 & 0.678 \\
hand-crafted (16-d)        & 0.644 & \textbf{0.711} & 0.672 & 0.544 & 0.060 & 0.942 \\
HuBERT-ECG (768-d)         & 0.174 & 0.134 & 0.016 & 0.195 & 0.000 & 0.899 \\
ECGFounder (1024-d)        & \textbf{0.885} & 0.709 & 0.860 & 0.851 & 0.137 & 0.983 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{figures/fig1_ari_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{PTB-XL SR-vs-AF clustering ARI by representation and clusterer (power scaling).
\rev{All 25 representation-by-clusterer cells on the cohort of 2\,542 records: ten seeds for
$k$-means, spectral clustering and the Gaussian mixture, and one deterministic fit for Ward
agglomerative and for HDBSCAN. Each cell gives the mean ARI over the half-width of its 95\%
patient-bootstrap interval.}}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

\rev{The patient-bootstrap intervals in the caption of Table~\ref{tab:main} separated every pair of representations except raw flattening from the conv-AE, whereas moving only the seed left the $k$-means partitions almost unchanged. A paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the $k$-means seeds confirmed every adjacent contrast at $p = 0.002$, the floor for ten paired samples (Section~\ref{sec:methods}). ECGFounder led the hand-crafted features by $\Delta = 0.242$ (paired patient-bootstrap interval [0.202, 0.280]), the hand-crafted features led HuBERT-ECG by $\Delta = 0.470$ ([0.424, 0.515]), and HuBERT-ECG led raw flattening by $\Delta = 0.174$ ([0.146, 0.203]). Cliff's delta and the matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation were both 1.00 on all three contrasts: every cross-seed pair and every signed rank favoured the representation placed higher in the ordering. The magnitudes are therefore read from the paired differences and their intervals.}

\rev{The ordering did not depend on fixing the cluster count to the number of classes. With adjusted mutual information for $K$ from 2 to 8, ECGFounder led at every $K$, from 0.825 at $K=2$ to a minimum of 0.441 at $K=7$ and 0.450 at $K=8$. The hand-crafted features fell monotonically from 0.557 to 0.329, HuBERT-ECG stayed at or below 0.132, and raw flattening never exceeded 0.005. Because HuBERT-ECG and raw flattening stayed low at every cluster count, their low ARI at two clusters is not an effect of the chosen $K$.}

\rev{The position of each representation followed from two things: how much of the label its space holds, which the supervised ceiling in the last column of Table~\ref{tab:main} measures, and where in the space that label lies. The hand-crafted representation is 16-dimensional and each coordinate is a quantity by which the classes are defined, RR irregularity and P-wave presence. Its ceiling was 0.942 and an unsupervised partition recovered 0.711. ECGFounder had the highest ceiling, 0.983, recovered the most, 0.885, and led at every $K$ from 2 to 8, so the rhythm split is one of the larger sources of variation in its 1024 dimensions. HuBERT-ECG had almost the same ceiling as the hand-crafted features, 0.899, and recovered 0.195. This difference between the two foundation models matches the report of \cite{lookingbeyond2026} that some ECG foundation embeddings organise by clinical label and others by dataset.}

\rev{Raw flattening and the conv-AE both clustered at chance level under all five clusterers, for different reasons. The conv-AE reached a supervised ceiling of 0.678, the class information that its bottleneck kept although it was trained only to reconstruct the signal, and its best clustering ARI was 0.008. Raw flattening reached a ceiling of 0.519 against a majority of 0.504, so a linear probe found almost no label in the 1992-dimensional vector. Two causes may explain this linear failure. The coordinates are amplitudes at fixed sample offsets, and no fixed offset falls on the same part of the cardiac cycle in two records. The vector also has nearly as many coordinates (1992) as the cohort has records (2\,542), which leaves a linear probe few records per coefficient. A random forest on the same vector reached 0.714, so a non-linear readout recovered label information that the linear probe missed. This is consistent with both causes, and the benchmark does not separate them.}

\subsection[Supervised separability and clustering]{\rev{Supervised separability and clustering}}

\rev{Fig.~\ref{fig:sep} plots the supervised ceiling of each representation against its clustering ARI, so that a representation that is separable but not clusterable falls far below parity. ECGFounder and the hand-crafted features were high on both properties. The conv-AE and HuBERT-ECG were separable but not clusterable, and raw flattening, with a linear ceiling barely above the majority baseline, was low on both. We read the gap of these weaker embeddings as one of geometry: their discriminative information exists but does not lie along their dominant axes of variation.}

\rev{A non-linear probe showed that this gap is not an artefact of a linear probe. A random forest on raw flattening reached accuracy 0.714, well above its linear ceiling of 0.519 and far above its clustering ARI of 0.000, so even a non-linear decoder recovered a label that clustering did not. The same probe gave 0.951 on the hand-crafted features, 0.983 on ECGFounder and 0.825 on HuBERT-ECG. On ECGFounder the two probes agreed to three decimals, the linear ceiling at 0.9831 and the random forest at 0.9835. On the two representations that cluster well the non-linear probe therefore added little over the linear ceiling (0.951 against 0.942 for the hand-crafted features), so their clustering is not held back by information that only a non-linear readout reaches. For HuBERT-ECG the random forest sat below the linear ceiling of 0.899, so probe linearity does not account for that gap either.}


\rev{The reading that the class information of the weaker embeddings lies off their dominant axes was tested out of fold. If it holds, a single direction estimated from other patients should recover the class. A linear discriminant was fitted on the training patients of a patient-grouped stratified five-fold split, the held-out patients were projected onto that single axis, and the projection was clustered with $k$-means without labels. The comparison arm was $k$-means on the same held-out records in their native representation. Fitting the discriminant on every label and then clustering the projection would recover the labels by construction, which is why the projected patients were held out. Over five folds and ten seeds, on 508 or 509 held-out records per fold, the mean ARI rose from 0.160 to 0.549 for HuBERT-ECG, from 0.003 to 0.121 for the conv-AE, from 0.646 to 0.758 for the hand-crafted features and from 0.893 to 0.923 for ECGFounder. It did not move for raw flattening, 0.000 to 0.001. These are held-out values, so the native column does not equal the full-cohort $k$-means ARIs of Table~\ref{tab:main}. The largest difference is 0.013, on HuBERT-ECG.}

\rev{HuBERT-ECG showed the largest gain. One direction estimated from other patients recovered more than three times what its own 768 dimensions yield unsupervised, so the class information is present, transfers across patients, and lies away from the directions that dominate the embedding. ECGFounder gained almost nothing, because its dominant axes already carry the split. The conv-AE rose off chance and stopped far short of its ceiling of 0.678, so its class structure is both off-axis and diffuse. Raw flattening did not move at all, which distinguishes its failure from that of HuBERT-ECG. The one direction tested, the linear discriminant estimated from other patients, did not separate its classes, so the label information that the random forest recovered from it appears not to lie along that direction.}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.60\linewidth]{figures/fig3_sep_vs_clust.pdf}
\caption[Supervised ceiling against clustering ARI.]{\rev{Supervised ceiling against clustering ARI per representation, on the cohort of 2\,542 records. Points far below parity are separable but not clusterable. All five clusterers are plotted for each representation. The filled marker is that representation's best clusterer and carries its 95\% patient-bootstrap interval, and the four open markers are the remaining clusterers, offset slightly along the ceiling axis so that they do not cover the interval bar. The dashed line is parity between the ceiling and the ARI.}}
\label{fig:sep}
\end{figure}

\subsection[Internal indices on degenerate solutions]{\rev{Internal indices on degenerate solutions}}

\rev{The silhouette, the one internal index we measured, rated a degenerate solution above a solution that recovered the classes. Raw flattening with agglomerative clustering attained a silhouette of 0.81 while the ARI was 0.000 and its largest cluster held a fraction of 1.00 of the cohort. That fraction is rounded to two decimals, so at most 12 of the 2\,542 records lay outside the largest cluster. The hand-crafted representation, which recovered the classes (ARI 0.711, balanced clusters), had a \emph{lower} silhouette of 0.28. Fig.~\ref{fig:trap} plots 157 clustering solutions, which cover 22 of the 25 representation-by-clusterer cells. The three cells left out are HDBSCAN fits that labelled the whole cohort as noise, for which the silhouette is undefined. Across the plotted solutions the degenerate one-cluster solutions attained a high silhouette at zero ARI. A degenerate one-cluster solution maximises compactness while carrying no class information. This is the artefact behind the original project's silhouette of 0.89, and it is why chance-corrected external metrics, read together with cluster-size sanity checks, are necessary.}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{figures/fig2_silhouette_trap.pdf}
\caption{\rev{Internal silhouette score against chance-corrected ARI, with marker size giving the
largest-cluster fraction.} The degenerate one-cluster solutions
\rev{(large markers, lower right)} attain high silhouette at zero ARI. \rev{The cohort of
2\,542 records at power scaling, one point per seed for $k$-means, spectral clustering and
the Gaussian mixture and one point for each deterministic Ward and HDBSCAN fit: 157 points
over 22 of the 25 representation-by-clusterer cells. The three HDBSCAN fits that label the
whole cohort as noise leave the silhouette undefined and are not plotted. Each point is a
single clustering solution, so the panel carries no interval.}}
\label{fig:trap}
\end{figure}

\subsection[Effect of feature scaling]{\rev{Effect of feature scaling}}

\rev{The mean ARI over clusterers depended on the feature scaling. For the hand-crafted features it was 0.629 under standard scaling and 0.606 under power scaling, but fell to 0.360 under robust scaling and 0.229 under quantile scaling. The effect was not confined to the hand-crafted features: the mean ARI of ECGFounder dropped from 0.866 under standard scaling to 0.093 under robust scaling. One possible cause for the hand-crafted features, which we did not test, is that robust scaling leaves the heavy tails of the arrhythmic RR statistics unbounded. A few outlying records could then dominate Euclidean distance. That explanation rests on arrhythmic quantities, which the coordinates of ECGFounder are not, so it does not account for the drop of ECGFounder, whose cause we did not examine. Feature scaling should therefore be reported in ECG clustering, together with a sweep over scaling methods.}

\subsection{Generalization to MIT-BIH beat morphology}

\rev{On the N-vs-V beat-morphology task of MIT-BIH the hand-crafted design again clustered far better than the conv-AE and the raw beat window (Table~\ref{tab:mitbih}). Its 21-dimensional beat-morphology feature set reached an ARI of 0.783, whereas the raw beat window reached 0.018 with $k$-means and 0.049 with agglomerative clustering, although the beats were R-peak aligned. The supervised ceiling of the raw beat window was 0.741 on this balanced task, against 0.925 for the beat-morphology features, and its clustering did not recover the label information that this ceiling shows. The conv-AE was again separable but not clusterable, with a supervised ceiling of 0.927 but a best ARI of only 0.127, from the Gaussian mixture. On this task the conv-AE clustered better than the raw beat window, whereas on PTB-XL the intervals of the two overlapped.}

