\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\rev{The practical reading is a decision rule for the two tasks evaluated here: SR-versus-AF rhythm separation on PTB-XL and N-vs-V beat morphology on MIT-BIH. If the data are balanced and come in the 12-lead format that the tested version of ECGFounder takes, ECGFounder gives the best unsupervised separation here. We tested two stress settings, a 9:1 SR:AF imbalance and a simulated non-IID federation over ten sites judged by its worst site draw. In both, the 16 hand-crafted features were the better choice, and they are also preferable where interpretability or low dimensionality matters. On balanced PTB-XL data their best ARI was 0.711 against 0.885 for ECGFounder. Under the 9:1 imbalance they overtook it (ARI 0.265 against 0.063), and over the site draws of non-IID federation they varied far less. The hand-crafted features remain the choice when the leads on hand are not the twelve that the tested foundation-model versions take. Neither of those versions could embed the 2-lead MIT-BIH beats, whereas the hand-crafted design carried over to them through a separate beat-level feature set of the same kind. The cost comparison of Table~\ref{tab:cost} supports the same rule. The representation that retains the most ARI under both stressors is also the less costly of the two to compute, and its 16 coordinates are clinical quantities that a reader can inspect for each record.}

\rev{Raw flattening and the conv-AE, including the design of the original project, recovered little of the arrhythmia classes without labels on either task. On PTB-XL both clustered at chance level under all five clusterers. On MIT-BIH their best ARIs were 0.127 for the conv-AE and 0.049 for the raw beat window, against 0.783 for the beat-morphology features. The statement on the conv-AE covers the one configuration tested, an autoencoder with a 32-unit bottleneck trained to reconstruct its input. Other bottleneck sizes, architectures and training objectives lie outside the scope of this study.}

\rev{Six things separate this study from the closest benchmark of ECG foundation models, which includes an analysis of how far the embeddings group by clinical label and by dataset \cite{lookingbeyond2026}:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Adding the hand-crafted features turns a comparison between foundation models into a comparison between representation families.
\item A supervised ceiling on every representation, linear and non-linear, tells a space that does not hold the label apart from one that holds it in the wrong geometry.
\item An out-of-fold test of that distinction, in which a discriminant fitted on other patients raises HuBERT-ECG from 0.160 to 0.549 in held-out ARI, shows that the second case occurs.
\item A second dataset and a second task type, the MIT-BIH beat morphology that benchmark omits, test whether the ordering of the representations depends on the task.
\item Two cautions that a practitioner can act on concern the degenerate silhouette and the dependence of the result on feature scaling.
\item Two deployment stressors, with the cost of each representation beside them, show where the ordering on balanced data stops being the useful answer.
\end{enumerate}
\end{revblock}

\rev{We limited the foundation-model embeddings to two models, selected for the reasons that follow rather than by a survey of the field.
HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder both publish weights that load into a frozen feature extractor
without fine-tuning, which a benchmark of frozen embeddings requires. They also differ
in pretraining objective and architecture: HuBERT-ECG is a self-supervised transformer
\cite{hubertecg2024}, and ECGFounder is a convolutional model pretrained on more than ten million
recordings \cite{ecgfounder2024}. Their clustering ARIs are far apart, 0.885 against 0.195, so the two do not behave as one kind of embedding. What this limit does to the conclusions is to scope them: every statement here about a foundation model
is a statement about one of these two, the deployment comparison is between the hand-crafted
features and ECGFounder specifically, and none of it extends to ECG foundation models as a
class. A further model that clusters as well as ECGFounder on balanced data would leave the
results reported here unchanged, and would have to be measured again under imbalance and
under federation before the deployment reading could be extended to it.}

\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

\rev{ECGFounder expects \mbox{500\,Hz} input, and because bulk access to the \mbox{500\,Hz} records was limited, the main benchmark embedded the \mbox{100\,Hz} cohort upsampled to the expected length. A check at \mbox{500\,Hz} used a 300-record subset in place of the full cohort. On that subset ECGFounder reached an ARI of 0.896 at native \mbox{500\,Hz} and 0.909 on upsampled \mbox{100\,Hz} input, a difference of 0.013 in favour of the upsampled input. The main benchmark may therefore overstate the ARI of ECGFounder by about that amount, which is small against its lead of 0.242 over the hand-crafted features.}

\rev{We evaluated two foundation models. We did not include others, among them ECG-FM, whose embeddings the benchmark of \cite{lookingbeyond2026} reported to separate clinical labels. Neither of the two was evaluated on MIT-BIH, for the reason given in Section~\ref{sec:methods}, so the MIT-BIH comparison covers the beat-morphology features, the conv-AE and the raw beat window only. The input of one of the two was also shorter than that of every other representation. HuBERT-ECG received the first \mbox{5\,s} of each \mbox{10\,s} strip and so saw fewer RR intervals, while every other representation saw the full strip. The shorter window is therefore an alternative explanation for its weak clustering, beside the off-axis geometry measured in Section~\ref{sec:results} and the organisation by dataset that \cite{lookingbeyond2026} reported for some foundation models.}

\rev{Both evaluated tasks are binary by design, one rhythm contrast and one morphology contrast, each chosen so that the class definition is unambiguous and a clustering failure can be charged to the representation rather than to label noise. No multi-class arrhythmia cohort was evaluated, and no result here is a statement about multi-class arrhythmia structure, where several rhythms share an irregularity signature and the largest class outnumbers every minority. A multi-class PTB-XL rhythm cohort over the same embeddings is the least costly way to test whether the ordering survives that setting.}

\rev{The AF class of the rhythm task also counted atrial flutter records as positives. The cohort took every record with an AFIB or AFLT statement as AF (Section~\ref{sec:methods}). The reading of Section~\ref{sec:intro} rests on AF being defined by an irregular rhythm. We did not check whether the flutter records share that irregularity, so those records may sit closer to the SR class than the AF definition suggests. The ARI of every representation was computed against these labels, and no result here separates the flutter records from the fibrillation records. Removing the AFLT records, or giving them a class of their own, would show how far they move the ARI of each representation.}

\rev{The federated study is a simulation on a single physical dataset, and no multi-institution deployment was evaluated. Its only control on heterogeneity is the Dirichlet concentration, which moves class prevalence across sites and nothing else, and the spread that alone produces is already wide: over the ten redrawn non-IID partitions ECGFounder ranged from chance to 0.881, while the hand-crafted features stayed between 0.455 and 0.643. Feature scaling in the simulation also used statistics pooled over all sites, which separate institutions would not share. The choice of scaling changed the centralized results, lowering the mean ARI of ECGFounder from 0.866 under standard scaling to 0.093 under robust scaling (Section~\ref{sec:results}). Device vendor, lead placement, population case mix and local labelling practice also differ between hospitals and are absent from the simulation. Settling their effect needs recordings held at several institutions, clustered under a federated protocol with the site of origin retained as a variable, which was not available for this study.}

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

\rev{For unsupervised arrhythmia structure in the ECG, the choice of representation moved clustering ARI further than the choice of clustering algorithm on the two tasks evaluated, and chance-corrected external evaluation was required to see it. On balanced PTB-XL data ECGFounder, pretrained on more than ten million recordings \cite{ecgfounder2024}, leads. Under the one class imbalance tested, a 9:1 SR:AF ratio, 16 interpretable hand-crafted features overtook it. Under simulated federation across ten heterogeneous sites they retained 0.92 of their centralized ARI against 0.69 and never fell below 0.455 over the ten site draws. On PTB-XL raw flattening and the conv-AE clustered at chance level under all five clusterers. Internal indices and feature scaling are not neutral choices and must be reported. Whether these results hold for multi-class arrhythmia cohorts, for other foundation models and for a real multi-institution federation is open.}

\section*{Code and data availability}
PTB-XL (version 1.0.3) \cite{ptbxl2020} and the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database (version 1.0.0) \cite{mitbih2001} are public
\rev{on PhysioNet \cite{physionet2000}.} The benchmark code, with the exact cohort
definitions, is available at \url{https://github.com/TheAxeC/ecg-representation-clustering}.

\section*{Ethics}
This study uses only de-identified, publicly available databases and required no new
human-subjects data collection.

